fund-csharp/13-equality-and-hashing/01-e.explanation:
Normalise once in the constructor; then every member compares the normalised `Value`, and `GetHashCode` uses the same value, so equal emails always land in the same bucket:

```csharp
public bool Equals(Email? other) => other is not null && Value == other.Value;
public override bool Equals(object? obj) => Equals(obj as Email);
public override int GetHashCode() => Value.GetHashCode();
public static bool operator ==(Email? a, Email? b) => a is null ? b is null : a.Equals(b);
```

Inside `operator ==`, test `a is null`, not `a == null`, or the operator calls itself forever. Writing it as plain `a.Equals(b)` throws a NullReferenceException on `none == null` and fails "Equals(object) and null handling". Hashing the RAW input instead of the normalised value fails "works as a HashSet and Dictionary key": the set ends up with two entries.

